## Solution

I use Duhamel's principle combined with Kirchhoff's formula for the 3D wave equation.

**Kirchhoff's formula (surface form).** For the homogeneous Cauchy problem
$$v_{tt}-\Delta v=0,\quad v(x,0)=0,\quad v_t(x,0)=g(x),$$
the solution in $\mathbb{R}^3$ is
$$v(x,t)=\frac{1}{4\pi t}\int_{|y-x|=t} g(y)\,dS_y=\frac{t}{4\pi}\int_{|z|=1}g(x+tz)\,dS_z.$$

*Verification of the surface form.* With $g\equiv 1$ this gives $v(x,t)=\frac{1}{4\pi t}\cdot 4\pi t^2=t$. Then $v_{tt}=0=\Delta v$, $v(x,0)=0$, $v_t(x,0)=1$. ✓ (The volume-integral version would give $t^2/3$, for which $v_{tt}=2/3\neq 0$, so it is **not** a solution of the homogeneous equation; the surface form is the correct one.)

**Duhamel's principle.** For the inhomogeneous problem
$$u_{tt}-\Delta u=F(x,t),\quad u(x,0)=0,\quad u_t(x,0)=0,$$
the solution is
$$u(x,t)=\int_0^t v(x,t-s;s)\,ds,$$
where for each fixed $s$, $v(\cdot,\tau;s)$ solves $v_{\tau\tau}-\Delta v=0$, $v(x,0;s)=0$, $v_\tau(x,0;s)=F(x,s)$.

**Applying to $F\equiv 1$.** By Kirchhoff with $g\equiv 1$,
$$v(x,\tau;s)=S(\tau)[1](x)=\tau \quad\text{for all }x\in\mathbb{R}^3.$$
Hence
$$u(x,t)=\int_0^t (t-s)\,ds=\frac{t^2}{2}.$$

**Verification.** $u(x,t)=t^2/2$ gives $u_{tt}=1$, $\Delta u=0$, so $u_{tt}-\Delta u=1$; $u(x,0)=0$, $u_t(x,0)=0$. All conditions are satisfied. By uniqueness of the Cauchy problem for the wave equation, this is the solution.

**Conclusion.** For every $x\in\mathbb{R}^3$ and every $t>0$,
$$u(x,t)=\frac{t^2}{2}>0,$$
so $u(x,t)\neq 0$. The statement is **true**.

